#!/usr/bin/env python
"""catl - `cat` for LLM readers.

Instead of dumping a large file, catl prints a compact, token-budgeted map of it
(outline, topics, key terms, cross-links) and ends with the exact commands a
reader LLM can run next: skip the file, open a node, read a line span, search,
grep, or follow links. The first run builds the map; later runs on the same
content are served from the cache:

    $MEMBASE_DIR/<sha[0]>/<sha[1]>/<sha[2:4]>/<sha256>/
        meta.json     name, kind, size, source paths (+ size/mtime), tools used, version
        map.json      nodes, segments, regions, topics, terms, links
        index.sqlite  contentless SQLite FTS5 index (inverted index only, no text)

The cache never copies the document. Zoom-in commands read the original file
from its recorded path, after checking it still has the same content (size +
mtime, falling back to a sha256 check). PDF/HTML/JSON text is re-derived from
the file the same way it was at build time, so line numbers stay stable. Only
stdin input, which has no file to go back to, is kept as stdin.bin.

MEMBASE_DIR defaults to ~/.membase. Cached documents can be addressed by hash
prefix (catl @3fa9c1d2 --open S2), so follow-up reads never rehash the source.

Runs on the Python standard library. Uses these when installed:
  pymupdf (fitz), pdftotext, pypdf   PDF text and bookmarks
  tree-sitter-language-pack          exact outlines for non-Python code
  drain3                             log template mining
"""
